<Project>
  <!--
    The AOT workaround, kept HERE rather than in every app that publishes with -p:Aot=true.

    Mono's WASM AOT mis-compiles an indirect call inside CupriFace method bodies, trapping at boot
    with "function signature mismatch". Retested on SDK 10.0.302 (2026-08): the symbolised fault is
    within SliderComponent.Expand's own AOT body (its first AngleSharp IElement call) — NOT the
    registry→component interface dispatch, so devirtualising the call site does not help (verified:
    the trap stays at the same frame with the caller changed). Interface calls made BEFORE
    expansion (ICupriComponent.Tag during Register) dispatch fine, so it is signature-specific Mono
    codegen rather than interface dispatch generally. Keeping CupriFace interpreted (the jiterpreter
    still applies) costs roughly 8x native on the measured showcase — hover restyle 16.2 ms/move,
    section switch 60.7 ms at 940x720 — while CoreLib, AngleSharp, Regex and the Skia bindings all
    stay AOT.

    Why it belongs to the package: this is a workaround for a defect in CupriFace's own code, and
    it was previously carried in consumers' csproj files, where nobody could tell them when it
    stopped being necessary. It is ours to retest on each SDK bump, and ours to delete.

    Applied only when the app actually AOT-compiles, so a plain build or `dotnet run` is untouched.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(RunAOTCompilation)' == 'true'">
    <_AOT_InternalForceInterpretAssemblies Include="CupriFace.dll" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
